Boundary Physics Physics
The generation rates due to the Universal Schottky Tunneling, GT (x) , are automatically
output to any saved structure files for every node. Additionally you can optionally output the
transmission coefficient to structure files by specifying the f lag UST.PROB o n th e OUTPUT
statement. This causes the quan tity between the node point and its corresponding
Schottky contact to be output for every node.
Examples
CONTACT NAME=ANODE WORKF=N.POLY SURF.REC F.USTELECDF=example.c
File example.c contains
#include <stdio.h>
#include <math.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <template.h>
int edfipe(int electrode,double tn,double energy,double
time,double *ge,double *dge)
{
   double arg;
   double darg;
   arg = pow(energy,3.0)/pow(tn,1.5);
   darg = 3.0*arg/energy;
   *ge=exp(-3.0e5*arg) + 2.5e-10*exp(-5.0e4*arg);
   *dge=-3.0e5*darg*exp(-3.0e5*arg) - 1.25e-5*darg*exp(-
5.0e4*arg);
   return(0);
}
The value of UST.ELECTEMP specied on a CONTACT statement is passed to this function via
the tn argument, and in this case is used to parameterise an ex pression for the electron
distribution function , returned as the *ge parameter. The derivative of the distribution
function with respect to energy must also be calculated, and is returned as the *dge parameter.
